WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.340
This video shows how to create a virtual machine on a shared Layer 2 network,

2
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.022
in the lab for OpenShift Virtualization on IBM Cloud.

3
00:00:09.022 --> 00:00:13.669
We work in the IBM Cloud console and in the OpenShift web console.

4
00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:17.391
At the end, we check that the virtual machine can reach the lab web page.

5
00:00:18.591 --> 00:00:22.388
Before we start, here is the network this virtual machine will use.

6
00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:24.760
It is a shared Layer 2 network.

7
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.982
It is made once for the cluster, and every lab project can use it.

8
00:00:28.982 --> 00:00:32.909
Virtual machines from different projects sit on the same network,

9
00:00:32.909 --> 00:00:35.554
and each one gets its address by DHCP.

10
00:00:35.554 --> 00:00:38.451
A router pod is the gateway for the network.

11
00:00:38.451 --> 00:00:41.725
It reaches the IBM Cloud VPC over VLAN 220,

12
00:00:41.725 --> 00:00:46.599
and a VPC route sends traffic for this range back to the router.

13
00:00:47.799 --> 00:00:50.521
We start in the IBM Cloud console.

14
00:00:50.521 --> 00:00:56.568
In the upper right, the account selector shows 2326338, ITZ-VMWARE.

15
00:00:56.568 --> 00:00:58.690
This is the account that holds the lab.

16
00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:00.412
Check it before you go on.

17
00:01:01.612 --> 00:01:05.184
Open the menu at the upper left, and choose Infrastructure.

18
00:01:05.184 --> 00:01:09.881
On the Infrastructure page, click OpenShift virtualization in the left menu.

19
00:01:09.881 --> 00:01:14.503
The page lists one cluster, virtualization-4399.

20
00:01:14.503 --> 00:01:15.725
We click its name.

21
00:01:18.312 --> 00:01:23.184
The cluster page has a blue button at the upper right, OpenShift web console.

22
00:01:23.184 --> 00:01:24.431
We click it.

23
00:01:24.431 --> 00:01:26.878
The console opens, already signed in.

24
00:01:29.111 --> 00:01:33.483
In the menu on the left, we open Virtualization, and click VirtualMachines.

25
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:35.980
The page lists projects on the left.

26
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.227
A new project has no virtual machines,

27
00:01:38.227 --> 00:01:42.302
so the filter Show only projects with VirtualMachines hides it.

28
00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:46.088
We turn the filter off, search for lab-39,

29
00:01:46.088 --> 00:01:49.874
and click our project, lab-4399-ph-lab-39.

30
00:01:51.074 --> 00:01:52.796
Now we click Create.

31
00:01:52.796 --> 00:01:55.293
A wizard opens, with six steps.

32
00:01:55.293 --> 00:01:57.690
The first is Deployment details.

33
00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:02.137
We type a name for the virtual machine: your initials, then layer2-vm.

34
00:02:02.137 --> 00:02:04.834
Ours is nt-layer2-vm.

35
00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:05.781
We click Next.

36
00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:09.496
Step 2 is Guest OS.

37
00:02:09.496 --> 00:02:13.218
We choose Other Linux, and choose centos.stream9 in the list.

38
00:02:13.218 --> 00:02:14.515
We click Next.

39
00:02:14.515 --> 00:02:16.837
Step 3 is Boot source.

40
00:02:16.837 --> 00:02:19.984
We select the volume centos-stream9, and click Next.

41
00:02:22.074 --> 00:02:24.671
Step 4 is Compute resources.

42
00:02:24.671 --> 00:02:26.618
We select General Purpose.

43
00:02:26.618 --> 00:02:30.273
The size starts as medium, so we open the size list,

44
00:02:30.273 --> 00:02:33.715
and choose small: 1 CPU and 2 GiB of memory.

45
00:02:33.715 --> 00:02:34.662
We click Next.

46
00:02:36.274 --> 00:02:38.646
Step 5 is Customization.

47
00:02:38.646 --> 00:02:42.243
Here we attach the virtual machine to the Layer 2 network.

48
00:02:42.243 --> 00:02:44.390
We open the Network tab.

49
00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.387
On the default interface, we click the kebab menu, and choose Edit.

50
00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:50.984
The Network list has three networks.

51
00:02:50.984 --> 00:02:53.731
We select the one with layer2 in the name.

52
00:02:53.731 --> 00:02:54.679
We click Save.

53
00:02:55.879 --> 00:02:57.851
We set a password here.

54
00:02:57.851 --> 00:03:00.098
We open the Initial run tab.

55
00:03:00.098 --> 00:03:02.745
In the Cloud-init section, we click Edit.

56
00:03:02.745 --> 00:03:04.642
The user is centos.

57
00:03:04.642 --> 00:03:08.814
We type a password in the password field, and it shows only as dots.

58
00:03:08.814 --> 00:03:10.086
Then we click Apply.

59
00:03:11.286 --> 00:03:14.083
We click Next to reach Review and create.

60
00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:16.880
The page shows the name and the project.

61
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.677
The box to start the virtual machine after creation is ticked.

62
00:03:20.677 --> 00:03:22.674
We click Create VirtualMachine.

63
00:03:23.886 --> 00:03:28.058
OpenShift copies the boot volume, and starts the virtual machine.

64
00:03:28.058 --> 00:03:32.080
The status changes from Provisioning, to Starting, and then to Running.

65
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.652
Soon after, the Network tile shows an IP address.

66
00:03:35.652 --> 00:03:38.649
The router in the cluster gave it, by DHCP.

67
00:03:40.527 --> 00:03:42.974
Now we open the Console tab.

68
00:03:42.974 --> 00:03:46.471
The guest login credentials are above the console.

69
00:03:46.471 --> 00:03:50.118
We type the user name, centos, and then the password.

70
00:03:50.118 --> 00:03:51.840
The password does not show.

71
00:03:56.777 --> 00:03:58.649
We run ip addr show.

72
00:03:58.649 --> 00:04:03.496
The interface eth0 has an address from the router, marked dynamic.

73
00:04:03.496 --> 00:04:06.443
It is the same address as the Network tile showed.

74
00:04:07.643 --> 00:04:11.140
Last, we run curl, with the address of the lab web page.

75
00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.412
The page answers, Well done, lab completed.

76
00:04:14.412 --> 00:04:18.109
It also names our address, so the traffic went through the pod router.

77
00:04:23.276 --> 00:04:26.730
You created a virtual machine on the shared Layer 2 network,

78
00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.073
gave it a password, and checked it from its console.

79
00:04:30.073 --> 00:04:34.145
When you have finished with a virtual machine, delete it, so that it frees its disk.

